course: dictionary
lesson: 9822
title: Sweeten: more than sugar
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To sweeten something is to make it taste sweeter, or to make it nicer. The word can be about food, like adding honey to porridge, but it can also be about feelings or deals. Read the clues around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: «Ryan moans about homework, so his teacher shows him a story from Sumer, about 4,000 years old, where a schoolboy gets punished for messy work and his dad has to sweeten the teacher.» The clues are punished and messy work, so the dad needs to make the teacher feel kinder again. Sweeten means to make someone feel happier or nicer toward you.
explain: Here is a test you can use. Put your meaning in place of the hard word and check it still makes sense. If you say «The fresh air made the room nicer», that fits «The fresh air sweetened the room». Try it with «sweetened» and see if it works.
fact: To sweeten food or drink means to make it taste sweeter, for example with sugar, honey or fruit.
fact: To make something more pleasant can also be to sweeten it: «The fresh air sweetened the room».
fact: To sweeten a deal or offer means to make it more attractive, often by adding something extra.
fact: Labels on drinks may say «sweetened» or «artificially sweetened», so read them carefully.
fact: The sentence «"Zero sugar" can be true while the drink is still sweetened» shows that a drink can have no sugar and still taste sweet.
exercise: To sweeten a cup of tea, what could you add? | A spoonful of salt | A splash of vinegar | A slice of raw onion | *A spoonful of honey
exercise: «The fresh air sweetened the room.» What does sweetened mean here? | Painted the walls a new colour | Made the room much darker | Made the room colder and wetter | *Made the room feel nicer and more pleasant
exercise: «"Zero sugar" can be true while the drink is still sweetened, coloured fizzy water.» What does sweetened mean here? | Is coloured bright red | *Has something added to make it taste sweet | Is served hot in a cup | Has had all its water taken out
test: Mum says, «I will sweeten the deal: an extra hour of play if you tidy your room.» What is she doing? | Asking you to buy her a sweet | Taking away your play time | *Making the offer more attractive by adding something extra | Ending the deal before it starts
test: Lena sweetens her porridge with fresh fruit instead of sugar. What does sweeten mean here? | To wash the bowl | *To make food taste sweeter | To make food colder | To burn the food
exam: «Ryan's dad has to sweeten the teacher.» Which meaning fits best here? | To make the teacher feel kinder and happier | To punish the teacher for messy work | *To make the teacher feel kinder and happier toward Ryan's family | To make the teacher's tea taste of sugar
